3 Claw Detection
In this section, we describe “claw-detection” algorithms that detect the existence of a claw. The claw-detection
algorithms will be used as subroutines in the “claw-search” algorithms presented in the next section that find a
claw.
    Before presenting the claw-detection
                                          algorithm, we introduce some notions. The Johnson graph J(n, k) is
a connected regular graph with nk vertices such that every vertex is a subset of size k of [n]; two vertices are
adjacent if and only if the symmetric difference of their corresponding subsets has size 2. The graph categorical
product G = (VG , EG ) of two graphs G1 = (VG1 , EG1 ) and G2 = (VG2 , EG2 ), denoted by G = G1 × G2 , is a graph
having vertex set VG = VG1 ×VG2 such that ((v1 , v2 ), (v′1 , v′2 )) ∈ EG if and only if (v1 , v′1 ) ∈ EG1 and (v2 , v′2 ) ∈ EG2 .
